Questions:
Title: Is gcc wrong to allow the initialization of a const array member with another array reference?.
Body: <p>While (re)implementing a simple constexpr map, I wrote this
(<a href="https://gcc.godbolt.org/z/6TnKn6zx4" rel="nofollow noreferrer">godbolt</a>):</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>template &lt;class key_type, class value_type, int N&gt;
class flat_map
{
private:
    struct pair
    {
        key_type key;
        value_type value;
    };
    const pair elements[N];

public:
    consteval flat_map(const pair (&amp;arr)[N]) noexcept
        : elements(arr) // works on gcc?!
    {}

    [[nodiscard]] consteval value_type operator[](const key_type key) const
    {
        for (const pair &amp;elem : elements)
            if (elem.key == key)
                return elem.value;
        throw &quot;Key not found&quot;;
    }
};

constexpr flat_map&lt;int, char, 3&gt; m = {{
    { 4, 'a' }, { -1, 'b' }, { 42, 'c' }
}};
static_assert(m[4] == 'a');
static_assert(m[-1] == 'b');
static_assert(m[42] == 'c');

int main()
{
    return m[4]; // 97=='a'
}
</code></pre>
<p>I naively thought to set the private array <code>elements</code> as <code>const</code> and initialize it in the constructor; I was using <em>gcc</em> trunk as compiler, and all was seemingly working well.
When I decided to try it with <em>msvc</em> and <em>clang</em>, I had compilation errors: both were complaining about the array initialization requiring a brace-enclosed initializer list.</p>
<p>In hindsight the other compilers aren't particularly wrong, are they?
Am I inadvertently using some <em>gcc</em> non standard extensions here?</p>
<p>Ehm, by the way, what would you do to avoid copying the array elements by hand?</p>

Answers:
None
You are correct that in C++11 and later, the initialization of a const array member with another array reference is not allowed. This is because C++11 introduced the rule of five (or the rule of three, depending on whether you're using old C++11), which states that if you have a class with at least five member functions, and one of them is a copy constructor, move constructor, copy assignment operator, move assignment operator, or destructor, then you must provide an implementation for all of them.

In your case, you have a class `flat_map` with a private member `elements` which is an array of pairs. The array initialization requires a brace-enclosed initializer list, which is not allowed for a const array member. Therefore, you cannot initialize the `elements` array with another array reference in the constructor.

If you want to use a const array member, you should remove the initialization in the constructor or make it non-const. Here's how you can modify your code:

```cpp
class flat_map
{
private:
    struct pair
    {
        key_type key;
        value_type value;
    };
    const pair elements[N];

public:
    flat_map() // remove initialization
        : elements(0) // initialize with 0 elements

    [[nodiscard]] consteval value_type operator[](const key_type key) const
    {
        for (const pair &elem : elements)
            if (elem.key == key)
                return elem.value;
        throw &quot;Key not found&quot;;
    }
};
```

Alternatively, you can also make the `elements` array non-const:

```cpp
class flat_map
{
private:
    struct pair
    {
        key_type key;
        value_type value;
    };
    const pair& elements[N]; // make it non-const

public:
    flat_map() // remove initialization
        : elements(nullptr) // initialize with nullptr

    [[nodiscard]] consteval value_type operator[](const key_type key) const
    {
        for (const pair &elem : elements)
            if (elem.key == key)
                return elem.value;
        throw &quot;Key not found&quot;;
    }
};
```

